Perhaps I just repeat myself, but let me state more clearly that if one
considers a general (or just Linux) patch for nethack.sh (as the subject
implies) $DISPLAY is a very poor choice.

Facts:

a) $DISPLAY is only a reliable indicator that the user wishes programs
that use X to use $DISPLAY.

b) NetHack is a program that MAY use X (on user's request)

There are a lot of scenarios where $DISPLAY is set, but the user is

- actually in Linux console VT with no X on that computer and wants to
play nethack on that computer

- running X and starting NetHack in an xterm to play it in classic
tty-mode, enjoying the anti-aliasing and other stuff (I do this, for
example)

- in a shell to a remote machine, where s/he naturally sets DISPLAY to
his/her home display (in case s/he does need some X program), but
understandably doesn't want to play NetHack that way.

- etc. and vice-versa

The only thing we can deduce from $DISPLAY alone is that if it is NOT
set, then NetHack shall not use X. Perhaps another safe bet is that if
we are a dumb terminal AND $DISPLAY is set, NetHack should use X. Any
more assumptions would require a direct connecion to the user's brain.
Or, if you think that's easier, some distinct options/commands. (As I
said earlier, these should IMHO be set-up by the user himself.)

